CAT = "o30Cranes"; SEG = "CRN"
Q = [
("A laborer spots a frayed sling during a crane lift. What does Subpart CC say the laborer should signal?",
 "Stop or emergency stop", ["Nothing; only signal persons signal", "Swing the load away from the crew", "Lower the load slowly to grade"],
 "1926.1417(y) says anyone who becomes aware of a safety problem must alert the operator or signal person with the stop or emergency stop signal, and the operator must obey it whoever gives it."),
("Under Subpart CC, who must make sure the ground under a crane is firm, drained and graded enough?",
 "The controlling entity", ["The company that rented out the crane", "The qualified rigger", "The signal person"],
 "1926.1402(c) places responsibility for adequate ground conditions on the controlling entity, the prime contractor or other party with authority over the site."),
("Besides shift and monthly checks, a crane in use must get a full inspection by a qualified person:",
 "Every 12 months", ["Every 5 years", "Every 3 months", "Every 36 months"],
 "1926.1412 requires a shift inspection and a monthly inspection by a competent person, plus an annual comprehensive inspection by a qualified person at least every 12 months."),
("On a wire rope eye made with U-bolt clips, the U section of each clip goes on:",
 "The dead end of the rope", ["The live end of the rope", "Alternating rope ends", "The thimble's outer curve"],
 "1926.251(c)(5)(i) requires the U section to contact the dead end, with the saddle on the live end. Riggers remember it as 'never saddle a dead horse'."),
("Which defect means a synthetic web sling must come out of service right away?",
 "Broken or worn stitches", ["Some faded webbing color", "Light surface dirt", "Stiffness from cold weather"],
 "1926.251(e)(8) lists acid or caustic burns, melting or charring, snags, punctures, tears or cuts, broken or worn stitches, and distorted fittings as reasons for immediate removal."),
("A sling is too long for the lift. Which fix is acceptable?",
 "Use a correct-length sling", ["Tie a knot in one leg", "Bolt the two legs together", "Wrap it twice around the hook"],
 "1926.251(c)(6) says slings shall not be shortened with knots, bolts or other makeshift devices, which cut the rated capacity sharply."),
("Rigging equipment must carry permanent, legible markings that show:",
 "Its recommended safe working load", ["The date it was bought", "The last inspector's name and date", "The weight of the last lift"],
 "1926.251(a)(2)(i) requires permanently affixed and legible identification markings, as the manufacturer prescribes, showing the recommended safe working load."),
("A 4,000-pound load hangs from a two-leg sling. Each leg is at 30 degrees from horizontal. About what tension is in each leg?",
 "4,000 pounds", ["2,000 pounds", "1,000 pounds", "8,000 pounds"],
 "Tension per leg is the load divided by two times the sine of the angle: 4,000 / (2 x 0.5) = 4,000 pounds. Flat sling angles multiply leg tension."),
("As the angle between a sling leg and the horizontal gets smaller, the tension in that leg:",
 "Increases", ["Decreases", "Stays the same", "Drops to zero"],
 "Leg tension equals the share of the load divided by the sine of the angle, and the sine shrinks as the angle flattens, so tension climbs quickly below about 45 degrees."),
("In buildings under construction, maximum safe floor load limits for storage areas must be:",
 "Posted in pounds per square foot", ["Painted in red on the columns", "Filed with the building owner", "Kept in the binder in the site office"],
 "1926.250(a)(2)(ii) requires maximum safe load limits of floors, in pounds per square foot, to be conspicuously posted in all storage areas, except slabs on grade."),
("Bags of cement stacked in tiers must be stepped back and cross-keyed at least every:",
 "10 bags high", ["5 bags high", "15 bags high", "20 bags high"],
 "1926.250(b)(4) requires bagged materials to be stacked by stepping back the layers and cross-keying the bags at least every 10 bags high."),
("Brick stacks on a construction site may be no taller than:",
 "7 feet", ["4 feet", "6 feet", "10 feet"],
 "1926.250(b)(6) limits brick stacks to 7 feet, and a loose brick stack must be tapered back 2 inches for every foot above the 4-foot level."),
("Masonry blocks stacked higher than 6 feet must be tapered back:",
 "Half a block per tier above 6 feet", ["Two inches per foot above 4 feet", "One full block every third tier", "Six inches at the very top tier only"],
 "1926.250(b)(7) requires the stack to be tapered back one-half block per tier above the 6-foot level. The two-inches-per-foot rule is for loose brick."),
("Lumber that will be handled by hand may be stacked no higher than:",
 "16 feet", ["20 feet", "12 feet", "10 feet"],
 "1926.250(b)(8)(iv) allows lumber piles up to 20 feet, but lumber to be handled manually may not be stacked more than 16 feet high."),
("Inside a building under construction, stored material must be kept at least how far from a hoistway or floor opening?",
 "6 feet", ["10 feet", "3 feet", "4 feet"],
 "1926.250(b)(1) requires 6 feet from any hoistway or inside floor opening and 10 feet from an exterior wall that does not extend above the top of the material."),
("An enclosed chute is required when debris is dropped outside the exterior walls from more than:",
 "20 feet", ["10 feet", "15 feet", "30 feet"],
 "1926.252(a) requires an enclosed chute of wood or equivalent whenever materials are dropped more than 20 feet to any point outside the exterior walls."),
("When debris is dropped through a floor hole without a chute, the landing area must be enclosed by barricades at least:",
 "42 inches high, 6 feet back", ["36 inches high, 3 feet back", "48 inches high, 10 feet back", "30 inches high, 4 feet back"],
 "1926.252(b) requires barricades not less than 42 inches high and not less than 6 feet back from the projected edge of the opening, with warning signs at each level."),
("Before maintenance under the raised body of a dump truck, the body must be held by:",
 "Its attached locking support", ["The truck's own hydraulic lift", "A coworker at the control lever", "A stack of loose wood blocks"],
 "1926.601(b)(10) requires dump bodies to have positive means of support, permanently attached and lockable, to prevent accidental lowering during maintenance or inspection."),
("Under 1926.601, vehicles in use on a site must have brakes, lights, horn and other safety parts checked:",
 "At the start of each shift", ["Once a week, on Monday mornings", "Once a month", "At each scheduled oil change"],
 "1926.601(b)(14) requires all vehicles in use to be checked at the beginning of each shift for service and parking brakes, tires, horn, steering, lights and other safety parts."),
("A construction danger sign must have which color as the main color of its upper panel?",
 "Red", ["Yellow", "Orange", "Green"],
 "1926.200(b)(2) requires red as the predominating upper panel color, with a black border and white lower panel. Danger signs are used only where an immediate hazard exists."),
]
